      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hydronephrosis is progressive distention of the renal pelvis resulting in compression and atrophy of the renal parenchyma secondary to ureteral outflow tract obstruction and associated increased hydrostatic pressure.",
              "pdf_page": 747,
              "printed_page": 665,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Most often hydronephrosis is unilateral (~80–85%) and occurs secondary to complete or partial obstruction of the ureter by uroliths, ureteral strictures, ureteral or trigonal neoplasia, retroperitoneal disease causing extraluminal compression, spay stump granuloma, trauma, radiotherapy, and accidental ligation of the ureter during ovariohysterectomy, cryptorchectomy, or after ectopic ureter surgery.",
              "pdf_page": 747,
              "printed_page": 665,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Bilateral hydronephrosis is less common, resulting from ureteral stone disease, congenital ureteral strictures, trigonal neoplasia, or a urethral outflow obstruction. Severe hydro nephrosis can also be seen in dogs with ectopic ureters (commonly males) associated with a ureterovesical junction (UVJ) stenosis.",
              "pdf_page": 747,
              "printed_page": 665,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
