      "subsections": {
        "DRUG(S) OF CHOICE": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DRUG(S) OF CHOICE",
          "canonical_heading": "DRUG(S) OF CHOICE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Success rate for medical management of obstruction is 8–13%.",
              "pdf_page": 747,
              "printed_page": 665,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "For a ureteral obstruction caused by uroliths—alpha-adrenergic blockade should be considered (cat: prazosin 0.25 mg/cat q12h; dog: 1 mg/15 kg q8–12h).",
              "pdf_page": 747,
              "printed_page": 665,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "IV fluid diuresis—being careful to avoid overhydration.",
              "pdf_page": 747,
              "printed_page": 665,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Diuretics, e.g., mannitol 0.25 g/kg bolus over 30 min; consider CRI (1 mg/kg/min) if effective.",
              "pdf_page": 747,
              "printed_page": 665,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hyperkalemia is often absent. Mild to moderate hyperkalemia often resolves with fluid replacement and/or bicarbonate administration unless bilateral obstruction and/or oliguria is present. Severe, symptomatic hyperkalemia requires prompt and aggressive medical or surgical management for emergent decompression.",
              "pdf_page": 748,
              "printed_page": 666,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
